{
  "verdict": "divergent",
  "divergences": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "The conclusion drops the outer B. Reinhardt's `T ⊢ B¬B Con q` translates to `𝓢 ⊢ K (∼(K conF))`, not `𝓢 ⊢ ∼(K conF)`. These are not interchangeable: if both systems are ordinary classical propositional logic, K is constantly ⊤, and conF is ⊥, all the Lean hypotheses hold, yet the Lean conclusion would require a proof of ∼⊤."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "The declared abstraction replaces the arithmetical language and its axiom schemas with sentence-level connectives and provability conditions. In particular, F need not be induced by a single formula q(x), and `F (∼σ)` represents the intended instance without expressing Gödel coding or the arithmetic operation ng."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Reinhardt defines Con q as a specific universal sentence over numbers. Lean requires only that conF provably imply each noncontradiction instance at a sentence; it does not characterize conF by those instances or require it to be the universal sentence."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "The boxed fixed-point biconditional has the correct modal scope, but its existence is an explicit hypothesis in Lean. In the source it follows from the fixed-point theorem and is not a separate condition on Theorem 6."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The sentence-by-sentence upper-bound hypothesis matches the source, but the conclusion does not. Reinhardt proves that the negation of known consistency is itself known; the TARGET asserts only the unboxed negation."
}
